 "notes": "Ultralight dark matter (ULDM) scalar-coupling limit, Section 6.1, Figure 14 top-middle panel (d_e = electron-mass coupling). NANOGrav's d_e is defined via Eq.(64) L includes -(phi/Lambda) sum_f d_f m_f fbar f with Lambda=M_Pl/sqrt(4pi), i.e. m_e -> m_e(1 + d_e kappa phi) with kappa=sqrt(4pi)/M_Pl. This is identical to the canonical Damour-Donoghue ScalarElectron d_me, so d_me = d_e(NANOGrav) with no conversion factor. Curve is the RED SOLID line = 'Correlated' signal at 95% credible level (the only curve shown for this panel; the uncorrelated/dashed variant is not drawn for d_e). Extracted from vector PDF figs/uldm/scalar_uldm_constraints.pdf via get_drawings; x/y log-axis calibration verified against tick decades (frame x 600.4-964.8 px = 10^-24 to 10^-20 eV; frame y 425.45-75.4 px = 10^-9 to 10^-2). Curve clipped to the paper's shown mass range m_phi in [1e-24,1e-20] eV (paper states constraints shown down to 1e-24 eV, extrapolatable flat to ~1e-26). The bound is a spiky sensitivity curve (frequency-bin dependent), envelope minimum d_me ~ 5e-8 near m_phi ~ 1e-23 eV, rising to ~1e-3 near 1e-20 eV. Assumes ULDM is the entire DM abundance at rho_phi=0.4 GeV/cm^3 (Table 1); bound scales as d*sqrt(rho_phi). Paper also bounds d_mhat(quark), d_mu, d_gamma(=ScalarPhoton d_e), d_g; the electron/muon/gluon couplings are highlighted as the strongest relative to lab bounds, electron listed first.",
